{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["(v. 9) <b>Who says… they shall instruct Your judgments…</b> This may be explained according to what our Sages of blessed memory said (Bava Batra 60a) concerning the incident where they came to judge a tree that was damaging the public road, etc., and he said to them, 'Wait until tomorrow,' etc., and he sent and cut it down, etc. — end of quote. And this is what the verse is saying: <b>'Who says'</b> etc. — such a person is fit to instruct judgment, etc., for there is no room to say to him, 'Take a beam,' etc. (Bava Batra 15b). And this is your sign: <b>'Who says to his father and to his mother.'</b>" "<b>Further,</b> it intends that since He commanded, <b>'Judges… for your tribes'</b> (Deuteronomy 16:18), and our Sages of blessed memory said (Sifrei) that one must appoint judges for every tribe, therefore it says that the tribe of Levi — given that we have established that they showed no favoritism toward father or brother or son — they are fit to be appointed over all the tribes of Israel. And this is what it says: <b>'they shall instruct Your judgments to Jacob'</b> in a general manner, for there is no concern that they would show favoritism to their own tribe more than to any other tribe."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "The references to Bava Batra concern a sage who pre-empted a legal judgment by personally removing a nuisance tree, and the dictum (Bava Batra 15b) that one who shows favoritism is unfit to judge. The verse signs are from Deuteronomy 33:9 itself."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:22:07.602777752Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}